1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:12,300
This is the adversary, the sceptical reviewer. It tries to break your result before a referee does: it re-runs your analysis and checks what your write-up claims.

2
00:00:12,700 --> 00:00:25,600
It re-runs your code rather than trusting the write-up, and looks for the classic faults: test data leaking into training, a result that seems too good, a step nobody can reproduce.

3
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:42,500
In a write-up, it checks that each cited paper is real and says what you claim. For a written-up prediction model, it goes through the reporting checklist a journal will use and names each item you missed.

4
00:00:42,899 --> 00:00:57,399
Its scope is how a result was produced and whether it holds up. Each finding is marked as checked, by re-running or reading, or as only suspected, and an unclear result is reported as unclear.

5
00:00:57,399 --> 00:01:14,700
When the work names a compound to make or data about to move, it asks the lawyer itself and includes the answer. The blacksmith builds the analysis, the artist draws the figures, and the bookworm finds the reading.

6
00:01:15,100 --> 00:01:25,200
A real run, on the made-up example that ships with Murmurent. A member asks whether colonies grown in serum really are larger than controls.

7
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:41,299
The answer is Questions. The number reproduces exactly, but the serum and control samples come from different countries, and the Methods describe a step the code never takes. The last line says no legal question arises.

8
00:01:41,700 --> 00:01:57,600
Say what to check and what worries you: leakage in a classifier, a manuscript against the checklist a journal will use, or whether the papers you cite are real and say what you claim.

9
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:13,700
To use it, ask Claude Code for the adversary and say what to check. Give it the code and the data, not a summary, because it runs them. The first line is the verdict: Pass, Questions or Reject.

10
00:02:13,700 --> 00:02:25,500
Its checklists cover prediction models and single-marker studies; for other study designs it names the right checklist but does not check it.
